# Test fixtures are byte-exact inputs, not text. Never let git translate them.
#
# Song identity in this project is SHA1(chart file bytes) and nothing else, so a
# line-ending translation on checkout does not just reformat a file — it moves a
# hash that tests assert and that the client would have to agree with.
#
# The concrete case, measured 2026-09-05: internal/sng/testdata/reference-notes.chart
# is the loose copy of the chart that also lives inside reference-sngcli-v0.3.0.sng.
# The archive is binary and cannot be translated; the loose copy was being committed
# LF-normalised. So TestReferenceChartRoundTripsByteExact compared CRLF bytes from
# the archive against LF bytes from disk. It passed on ENG-1, where the working tree
# still held the CRLF original (sha1 30b18fee1d336a6b83c2fd7e134487d013710e14), and
# would have failed on any fresh Linux checkout, where the blob checks out as
# sha1 15ba37a4812c74ad523ddcd614332efc598c3029 — that is the machine CI, the Docker
# build and the Pi all run on. The failure reads as a reader bug, not as a line
# ending, which is what makes it worth pinning here rather than remembering.

# EVERY text file is LF in the working tree on every machine, not just in the
# index. A per-extension list was tried first and was not enough: it missed
# `LICENSE`, which has no extension, and `web/browse.html`, which is `go:embed`ed
# into the server binary. That second one is not a tidiness problem —
#
#   **the browse page SERVED TO USERS had different bytes depending on which
#   operating system built the binary.** 15,653 bytes from a Windows build,
#   15,302 from Linux, same commit. Content-Length differs, and any future ETag
#   over it would too.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git detect binary content and leave it alone, and the
# testdata rule below overrides this for the fixtures whose bytes are hashed.
* text=auto eol=lf

**/testdata/** -text

# The three recurring costs the CRLF checkout was causing, all of which look
# like something else:
#
#   1. `gofmt -l .` flagged nine files that were perfectly formatted, so the one
#      real formatting error would have been invisible in the noise.
#   2. `bash ci/*.sh` died on `$'\r'`, which reads as a broken script.
#   3. Five files sat permanently "modified" with an EMPTY diff. That made the
#      tree dirty to `git describe --dirty`, and Go stamps `vcs.modified=true`
#      into every binary it builds from a dirty tree. Measured 2026-09-08:
#      that flag was the ENTIRE remaining difference between a local build and
#      a CI build of the same commit, after the toolchain and `-trimpath` were
#      lined up - 2,916 differing bytes out of 8.5 MB, all of it the VCS stamp
#      and the Go build ID derived from it.
#
# Test fixtures stay `-text` above and are unaffected; this is only about files
# whose bytes nobody hashes.
